WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.790
Where your money goes: value chains, market channels and your real costs.

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.430
This is the video for module one of Agribusiness and Market Access.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:20.100
Every crop you sell travels a road before someone eats it. This road, with all its people, is called the value chain. It starts with you, the farmer.

4
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:28.250
An aggregator, sometimes called a middleman, buys small amounts from many farmers and gathers them into a truckload.

5
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.120
A wholesaler buys in bulk at a big market.

6
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:42.030
Then a processor or a retailer, and finally the household that eats it. At each step someone adds work and risk, and the price rises.

7
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:51.410
Is the middleman simply stealing the difference? Let us look. These are illustrative numbers to show the method, not today's prices.

8
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:58.100
Hauwa receives 12,000 naira a basket, 480,000 naira in total.

9
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:05.150
The aggregator pays about 120,000 naira more for transport, loading and levies.

10
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:16.920
On the way, 6 baskets are crushed or spoil in the heat. He sells only 34, at 22,000 naira each, for 748,000 naira.

11
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:33.870
His margin is 148,000 naira, before his time and his risk. So most of the gap is transport, levies and lost baskets. Hauwa's best levers are cutting losses, sorting better produce, and selling in bulk with others.

12
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.710
Pick the crop that brings you the most money. Write who bought it last season, the unit, and the price you received.

13
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:53.540
Politely ask that buyer where it goes next. Then phone someone at that step, or your state ADP extension office, and ask what they pay for the same unit and quality.

14
00:01:54.790 --> 00:02:02.220
Note the costs in between: transport, levies, bags, losses. Then draw the chain with a price under each person.

15
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:07.310
Farm prices change from week to week, so always write the date.

16
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:16.370
Never compare the offered price. Compare the net price, what you keep after transport, levies, loading and rejected produce.

17
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:25.720
The farm gate offer of 55,000 naira a bag has no costs. Blessing keeps 1,100,000 naira.

18
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:36.510
The weekly market pays 62,000, but selling costs 2,000 a bag. She keeps 1,200,000 naira, with same-day cash.

19
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:53.520
The feed mill pays 66,000, minus 3,000 transport. It rejects 2 wet bags, which she sells later for less. She still keeps the most, 1,234,000 naira. But payment comes 14 days later.

20
00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.800
If school fees are due tomorrow, the weekly market may suit her better.

21
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:08.410
When you sell to a new buyer, agree how and when you will be paid before anything is loaded. Watch the weighing, and ask what happens to produce they reject.

22
00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:20.770
Do not release produce because of an SMS credit alert or a screenshot. Fake alerts are a common trick. Open your own bank or mobile money app and confirm the money has arrived.

23
00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:35.120
You cannot tell whether a price is good if you do not know what the crop cost you. Count everything: land, ploughing, seed, fertiliser, hired labour, your own family's days, shelling, bags and transport.

24
00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.740
Amina's season costs 850,000 naira.

25
00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:46.180
She divides by her 25 bags. Her cost is 34,000 naira a bag.

26
00:03:47.430 --> 00:04:01.660
For a 20% margin, she multiplies by 1.20. Her minimum is 40,800 naira a bag. Now, when a trader offers 38,000, she knows she covers costs but misses her target.

27
00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:12.890
To remember. Much of the price gap is transport, levies and losses. Map your chain with dated prices. Compare the net price, never the offered price.

28
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:19.980
Confirm payment in your own app before loading. And know your cost per unit, because every decision depends on it.

29
00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:24.240
The common mistake is forgetting family labour, land and bags.

30
00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:37.670
Now practise. Map one value chain with dated prices. Compare the net price of two channels. Build your cost sheet, and complete the readiness check. Circle your two weakest items.

31
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.960
Continue with the lessons below.
